      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htChartMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues the teachings on how to add negative numbers and represent them. Therefore, we suggest studying the previous text (<htcite0>) before proceeding.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1d69a491-3e55-4eec-abee-70374df66efc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Modulating (Part I)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_Introdction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we add negative numbers, we calculate in exactly the same way as when we add positive numbers. As a consequence, in this scenario, a sum is like walking along a path such as the Peabirú in America (*Abya Yala*) (**Figure 1**) (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1d69a491-3e55-4eec-abee-70374df66efc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Modulating (Part I)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img src=\"\" id=\"imgPeabiru\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgPeabiru', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Representation of the Peabirú path followed by Aleixo Garcia. © Mapswire (<htcite0>). Shared under the <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0) license.</a></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The fact that we walk the same way regardless of direction brings another analogy already used in *History Tracers*. This time, it relates to performing the same operation repeatedly — that is, multiplying (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Multiplying There as Here</b><br /><br />Multiplication is the repeated addition of the same number (<htcite0>). Can we say that we multiply to the left just as we do to the right, with the difference that, on the left, the results keep a negative sign?</div></div></center>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_remembering",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Remembering\n\nLet us review the conclusions we reached about multiplying positive numbers before taking our first steps with negative numbers.\n\n#### The sum\n\nFirst, we saw that, when we take 10 steps to the right, we can represent those steps as follows (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>10</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Sum of all ten steps taken to the right.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A new representation\n\nIn the same text, we also saw that we could rewrite the repeated sum of the values as follows (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>10 × (1)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>10</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Sum of all steps.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Tabulating\n\nAs we continued our studies, we saw that, because certain results occur often, we can list them in a table using the following formula (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6428e19-afcf-4d3b-916d-46e5a332ef53",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math displaystyle=\"false\" mathsize=\"1.5em\"><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Result of multiplying by one.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "By using **Formula 1**, we obtained the following table, which we named the **1 Times Table** (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6428e19-afcf-4d3b-916d-46e5a332ef53",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent1\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Finally, we learned that we can plot a graph of the numbers we multiply (<htcite0>). If we connect the points, they form a straight line (**Graph 1**):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6428e19-afcf-4d3b-916d-46e5a332ef53",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart4\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Points of the 1 times table connected, forming a straight line. Graph made with the <htcite0> library.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_mirror",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying to the left\n\nThe process of multiplying to the left is exactly the same as the one we remember. We will still repeat a number the same number of times; the only difference is that, because we are moving in the opposite direction, we use the negative sign. To make this comparison clearer, we will rewrite the same steps we performed for positive numbers, now for negative numbers.\n\n#### The sum\n\nSince we are walking to the left, we add negative numbers:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>-10</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Sum of all ten steps taken to the left.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A new representation\n\nJust as we rewrote the repeated sums to the right, we can also rewrite them to the left:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>10 × (-1)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>-10</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Sum of all steps to the left.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Notice that in **Calculation 4**, even though the number of steps is positive (10), we keep the negative sign to show that we are walking to the left.\n\n#### Tabulating\n\nTo build the multiplication table, we will make a small modification to **Formula 1**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math displaystyle=\"false\" mathsize=\"1.5em\"><mi>-N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>-1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Result of multiplication by -1.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we use **Formula 2** to build the multiplication table, we must not forget to add the negative sign to the result, which indicates the direction we are walking:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent2\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: What about the graph?</b><br /><br />We saw that the negative sign indicates we are going in the opposite direction. What happens when we multiply by negative numbers?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Looking at **Graph 1**, we see that as we move away from zero to the right, the result moves away from the horizontal line upward. When we multiply by negative numbers, we still add the same number repeatedly, so the graph must also move away from the horizontal line — but in the opposite direction compared with positive numbers: downward.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart5\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: Points of the -1 multiplication table connected, forming a straight line. Graph made with the <htcite0> library.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_unifying",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Unifying\n\nOne possible translation for *Peabirú* is “round-trip path” (<htcite0>) — a fact that shows we can travel it in two directions.\n\nLooking at **Graphs 1 and 2**, we see that **Graph 1** starts at point zero, its minimum, and rises as we move to the right. **Graph 2** is the mirror image: zero sits at its far right end, its maximum, and the line falls as we move to the left.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "035560e0-0a4b-4b81-9df6-6c14cf429e81",
                     "text": "Alomía-Lucero, J. M., & Sixto-Dávila, L.",
                     "page": "pp. 5",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: What about the graph (Part II)?</b><br /><br />Is it possible to join the two graphs?<br /><br />If you answered “yes”, how would you do it?<br /><br />If you think it is not possible, why did you reach that conclusion?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Reflecting a bit further\n\nIf you concluded that the two graphs cannot be unified, that only shows that some ideas are still unclear. Let us help you deepen them.\n\nNotice that in **Formulas 1 and 2** we always multiply the number *N* by values that do not change — that is, constants. In one formula the constant is 1; in the other, it is -1.\n\nInstead of keeping two different formulas for the multiplication tables, we can adopt a more general one. Rather than considering *N* only as positive numbers, we can treat it as either negative or positive, depending on its position on the horizontal axis of the graphs. The constant, however, always stays 1. With that, we can reuse **Formula 1** by removing the restriction that *N* must be greater than zero — and, as a result, we obtain **Graph 3**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart1\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 3</b>: Points of the 1 times table connected, forming a straight line. Graph made with the <htcite0> library.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "One final point worth emphasizing is that the line connecting the points in **Graph 3** comes from multiplying the numbers that lie between the values — a topic first studied in *History Tracers* (<htcite0>) and explored in depth in later texts.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "010490cd-6da9-4992-a765-8a03188da892",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
